=== layout: section

# Start Simple: Three Rhinos

=== layout: one-panel

# Step 1: Get the Sequences

@body

- Northern white rhinoceros: N.Wh.
- Southern white rhinoceros: S.Wh.
- Black rhinoceros: Blk.R

![Rhino example](assets/lect11c_rhino/aligned.drawn.svg)

Use these example protein fragments to count amino acid differences.

=== layout: one-panel

# Step 2: Align the Sequences

@body

![Rhino example](assets/lect11c_rhino/aligned.drawn.svg)

Compare letters in the same column. Each fragment has 30 amino acids.

=== layout: one-panel

# Step 3: Replace Matches With Dots

@body

![Rhino example](assets/lect11c_rhino/dots.drawn.svg)

A dot matches the northern-white reference. A letter marks a difference from that reference.

=== layout: subsection

# Build the Distance Matrix

=== layout: one-panel

# Step 4: Set Up the Distance Matrix

@body

{table-style=comparison border="0.5pt solid #C5CBD2" table-group=rhino-three padding="3pt 2pt" font-size=22pt}
|  | N.Wh. | S.Wh. | Blk.R |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| N.Wh. | 0 | ? | ? |
| S.Wh. | ? | 0 | ? |
| Blk.R | ? | ? | 0 |


Three sequences need four rows and four columns, including labels.

The diagonal is zero: each sequence matches itself.

=== layout: one-panel

# Count Differences: The White Rhinos

@body

![Rhino example](assets/lect11c_rhino/dots.drawn.svg)

How many positions differ between N.Wh. and S.Wh.?

